# Trivy IaC-scan suppressions for terraform/.
#
# Every entry below is a DELIBERATE, ADR-ratified configuration choice for this
# SHORT-LIVED, SINGLE-OPERATOR EKS *validation* cluster — not an unreviewed risk.
# Each is justified inline and cross-referenced to the ADR that ratifies the
# trade-off. Trivy still FAILS CI on any OTHER CRITICAL/HIGH finding, so this file
# is a triage record, not a blanket mute (see
# .github/workflows/ci.yml § terraform-validate and ADR-019).
#
# Re-triage this list whenever the network / EKS / IAM configuration changes.
# For a persistent or production cluster, the correct action is to REMEDIATE these
# (narrow the API CIDR, disable the public endpoint, add a KMS CMK) rather than to
# keep suppressing them.

# AVD-AWS-0041 (CRITICAL) — "EKS cluster should not have open public access CIDRs."
#   var.cluster_endpoint_public_access_cidrs defaults to 0.0.0.0/0 so a first-run
#   operator can reach the Kubernetes API with kubectl from a workstation while
#   validating a throwaway cluster. It is a documented knob the operator MUST
#   narrow to their own IP/CIDR for any real environment, and private endpoint
#   access is enabled alongside it. See ADR-017 (endpoint posture) and the
#   variable's own guidance in terraform/variables.tf.
AVD-AWS-0041

# AVD-AWS-0040 (HIGH) — "EKS cluster should not have public access enabled."
#   Same rationale: the public API endpoint is intentionally on for out-of-band
#   operator validation of a short-lived cluster, restricted by the CIDR knob
#   above and paired with private access. Set cluster_endpoint_public_access =
#   false for a private-only production posture. See ADR-017.
AVD-AWS-0040

# AVD-AWS-0039 (HIGH) — "EKS should have secrets encryption (KMS envelope) enabled."
#   No customer-managed KMS envelope encryption of Kubernetes secrets is
#   configured on this validation cluster: it stores no production secrets (the
#   MLflow credential is delivered out-of-band at run time), and it is destroyed
#   after evidence capture. Adding a CMK is the documented hardening step for a
#   persistent/production cluster. See ADR-017 and ADR-019.
AVD-AWS-0039

# AVD-AWS-0164 (HIGH) — "Subnet associates public IP address."
#   Flags map_public_ip_on_launch = true on the two PUBLIC subnets
#   (aws_subnet.public). This is intentional and correct: public subnets exist to
#   host the NAT gateway and future public load balancers and must auto-assign
#   public IPs by definition. The EKS WORKER NODES — the actual workload — run in
#   the PRIVATE subnets (aws_subnet.private), which leave this unset (false) and
#   have NO public IPs; nodes reach the internet outbound only via NAT. This is
#   the standard EKS public/private VPC split ratified in ADR-015, not exposure of
#   the workload. Remediating would mean rearchitecting PR 2's network — out of
#   scope for a CI-validation PR.
AVD-AWS-0164
